[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Appchains, Subnets, and Layer 3 Custom Architectures"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As Web3 scales, the monolithic blockchain model struggles with high gas fees, throughput bottlenecks, and limited customization. Developers are increasingly turning to dedicated execution environments known as Appchains, Subnets, and Layer 3 (L3) architectures. This tutorial explores the theoretical foundations and practical implementation approaches for deploying these custom networks."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Scalability Trilemma in Custom Architectures"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Traditional blockchains bundle consensus, data availability, execution, and settlement into a single layer. Layer 2 rollups optimize execution and settle to an L1, but still share blockspace with other decentralized applications. Appchains and L3s take this a step further by offering dedicated blockspace, customizable state transition functions, and optimized validator sets."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Appchains: Independent blockchains optimized for a single application, providing complete sovereignty over gas tokens, consensus mechanisms, and governance."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Subnets: Dynamic sets of validators working together to achieve consensus on the state of a custom blockchain, prominent in ecosystems like Avalanche."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Layer 3s: Custom networks built on top of Layer 2 rollups, leveraging L2 for data availability and settlement while providing hyper-scalability and low costs."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring a Custom L3 Rollup Stack"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building an L3, developers typically use modular frameworks such as Arbitrum Orbit or Optimism OP Stack. Below is an example configuration file for initializing a custom Orbit L3 chain using JSON-RPC parameters."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"{
  "chainId": 412346,
  "chainName": "CustomAppChainL3",
  "rpcUrl": "http://localhost:8547",
  "nativeCurrency": {
    "name": "CustomToken",
    "symbol": "CTK",
    "decimals": 18
  },
  "parentChainId": 421614,
  "bridgeAddress": "0x1234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef12345678"
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Deploying Smart Contracts on Dedicated Execution Layers"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once your custom network is running, you can deploy smart contracts using standard Ethereum tooling like Hardhat or Foundry. Ensure you update your configuration files to point to your new L3 or Subnet RPC endpoint."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// Hardhat network configuration for custom L3
module.exports = {
  networks: {
    customL3: {
      url: 'https://rpc.customappchain.io',
      accounts: [process.env.PRIVATE_KEY],
      chainId: 412346
    }
  },
  solidity: '0.8.20'
};"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion and Best Practices"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Designing Appchains, Subnets, and Layer 3 architectures offers unmatched scalability and customizability. However, it introduces bridging risks and fragmentation of liquidity. Always implement robust cross-chain messaging protocols, secure validator sets, and thorough testnet simulations before deploying your custom architecture to production."}]
